# Saltline Dub

**Ingredients:** EGYPTIAN SIMSIMIYYA + DUB TECHNO + PREPARED HARMONIUM + RIQQ + BASS FLUGELHORN + TAPE ECHO

**Creative Catalyst:** The genre began during a winter recording residency inside a retired canal-signalling office in Port Said, where simsimiyya player Mariam El-Sayed rehearsed short plucked cycles beneath old maritime relay panels while Berlin producer Jonas Kern tested dub-techno chord sequences through a compact analog mixer. The connection became useful when Jonas stopped looping Mariam’s playing and instead treated each hand-played cycle as a moving harmonic shoreline. The simsimiyya did not trigger the electronics; both layers drifted independently, meeting only at pre-agreed tonal landmarks. Prepared harmonium, riqq, bass flugelhorn, and tape echo were added to create a form where acoustic repetition and electronic repetition occupy different lengths, causing their alignments to change continuously without changing tempo.

Saltline Dub is a slow, tensile acoustic-electronic form built around cyclic misalignment. Dub techno provides the spatial chassis: muted four-on-the-floor pressure, filtered chord stabs, deep but restrained bass, long delay feedback, spring or plate reverb, and gradual spectral change across extended sections. The Egyptian simsimiyya provides a contrasting rhythmic-harmonic system through bright plucked attacks, repeated modal figures, open-string resonance, and hand-shaped phrases that naturally breathe outside strict machine loop lengths.

The simsimiyya is recorded with one close small-diaphragm condenser aimed toward the soundboard, one ribbon microphone near the lower strings, and a restrained room microphone. The close mic captures finger attack, string separation, buzzing bridge detail, and damping. The ribbon provides body and lower resonance. The room mic is kept narrow and low so the instrument remains intimate rather than touristically expansive.

Players favor two- to seven-note cells, open fifths, modal thirds, repeated tonic figures, drones, short descending turns, octave reinforcement, muted ghost plucks, and brief tremolo. Long virtuosic runs are rare. The defining compositional behavior is that a phrase may last five, seven, nine, or eleven pulses while the electronic dub loop remains four or eight beats long.

The central compositional device is the **Mooring Cycle**. The acoustic and electronic layers are written as separate repeating lengths that share the same tempo but not the same restart point. A seven-pulse simsimiyya figure may repeat against an eight-beat dub-techno pattern. A five-note harmonium breath may span three bars while the bass remains in four-bar cycles. The music develops through the changing moments where these independent loops temporarily align, then slide apart again.

Unlike trigger-based hybrid forms, Saltline Dub avoids event-for-event response mapping. No kick is required to answer a pluck, and no synth chord is required to follow a specific acoustic cue. The ensemble instead listens for convergence points. Some are emphasized with silence, others are allowed to pass unnoticed. This creates long-form movement without conventional chord progression.

Dub-techno drums remain restrained. Kicks are short, low, and warm, usually four-on-the-floor but periodically removed for one or two beats. Snares and claps are sparse. Hats are narrow and filtered. Percussion is more often implied through delay returns, riqq articulation, and chord-envelope rhythm than through dense programmed layers.

The chord system uses an analog or virtual-analog polysynth with compact minor-seventh, suspended-second, open-fifth, and fourth-stack voicings. Each chord is short at the source but sent into long tape or digital delay. Filter cutoff, pre-delay, and feedback change gradually over eight, sixteen, or thirty-two bars. The dry chord remains modest; the evolving echo field carries most of the architecture.

Prepared harmonium provides a physical sustained layer. Selected reeds are partially muted with reversible felt or paper shutters, producing uneven air, soft beating, and shortened resonance. It plays narrow drones, open fifths, suspended seconds, and two-note clusters. Its parts are intentionally longer than both the simsimiyya and machine loops, creating a third independent timescale.

Riqq provides dry acoustic detail. Finger taps, muted jingles, rim strokes, thumb rolls, and single open jingles are used sparingly. The riqq does not replicate traditional accompaniment patterns continuously. Instead, it inserts short asymmetrical figures that make the overall cycle feel hand-played even when the kick remains exact.

Bass flugelhorn supplies low-mid brass warmth without fanfare. It plays breath-led fundamentals, short fifths, low minor thirds, half-valve smears, and soft held notes. It enters mostly in sections where the simsimiyya is sparse, acting as a slow-moving human air column against the electronic low end.

Bass remains mono and disciplined. A round analog sub or electric bass synth plays mostly roots, fifths, and occasional octave drops. Notes are short enough to leave space for tape feedback and low simsimiyya resonance. Long dub bass lines are possible, but they must avoid crowding the Mooring Cycle.

Tape echo is central but controlled. Delay times are not constantly synchronized. One return may be set slightly shorter than a dotted quarter, another slightly longer than a half note. The resulting phase drift creates moving rhythmic shadows without granular processing or obvious glitch edits. Feedback rises slowly and is manually reduced before saturation obscures the source.

The defining tension is fixed tempo versus floating recurrence. Everything moves at one speed, but not everything returns home at the same time.

## Genre DNA

**Tempo & Pulse:** 108–120 BPM, usually 4/4. Warm dub-techno kick, sparse filtered hats, occasional missing beats, dry riqq accents, and acoustic cycle lengths of 5, 7, 9, or 11 pulses layered against 4- and 8-beat electronic loops.

**Harmonic Palette:** Nahawand- and Bayati-adjacent modal colors used non-dogmatically, open fifths, minor 7ths, suspended 2nds, fourth stacks, pedal tones, modal thirds, and slow bass-root movement. Harmony changes gradually while cycle alignment creates perceived motion.

**Production Signatures:**

1. **Mooring Cycle** — acoustic and electronic patterns repeat at different lengths while sharing one tempo, causing alignments to drift and recur.
2. **Unsynced Echo Pair** — two delay returns use related but intentionally non-identical times, creating slow rhythmic phase movement.
3. **Dry Pluck / Wet Field** — simsimiyya remains mostly close and dry while electronic chords and selected harmonium notes occupy the long spatial field.
4. **Third-Timescale Harmonium** — prepared harmonium phrases are longer than both the simsimiyya cycle and drum loop, creating a third structural layer.
5. **Riqq Interruption** — riqq appears in brief hand-played figures rather than continuous accompaniment, disturbing machine regularity without destroying pulse.
6. **No-Trigger Rule** — instruments are not assigned one-to-one reactive functions; development comes from independent cycles meeting and separating.

**Dynamics:** Intensity grows through higher delay feedback, denser simsimiyya tremolo, stronger riqq attacks, wider synth-filter openings, thicker harmonium reed combinations, and more frequent brass entrances. Climaxes remain deep and suspended rather than explosive.

## Scene & Origin Lore

Saltline Dub formed inside a retired canal-signalling and shipping-record office in Port Said. The building retained green metal cabinets, brass telegraph labels, route diagrams, faded vessel logs, ceramic insulators, wooden desks, relay boxes, coiled signal wire, salt-clouded windows, and narrow balconies overlooking the canal traffic lanes.

Simsimiyya player **Mariam El-Sayed** had been using the building to document several short modal cycles associated with coastal performance traditions. Her interest centered on how repeated figures changed when one extra pluck was added or removed, especially when the pattern continued long enough for the listener to lose track of the starting point.

Berlin producer **Jonas Kern** was working in the adjacent signal room on stripped dub-techno material. His first attempt synchronized one recorded simsimiyya phrase to an eight-bar sequence. The result felt stable but generic. Mariam instead played a seven-pulse phrase continuously while Jonas left his loop at eight beats. The relationship changed every repetition even though neither part altered.

Prepared-harmonium player **Nadia Farouk** joined after discovering that long uneven reed drones could pass across multiple convergence points without resetting. Riqq player **Youssef Halim** added short hand-played interruptions that made the machine rhythm feel less absolute. Bass-flugelhorn player **Lea Hartmann** completed the group with low breath tones that could bridge wide empty sections without functioning as lead melody.

Their first performances were called **mooring charts**. A wall diagram showed several horizontal cycle lengths as parallel lines with small marks where they temporarily aligned. The chart was not a score of notes; it was a map of recurrence. Performers were free to thin or thicken their parts as long as the underlying loop lengths stayed intact.

The visual language developed around oxidized brass, canal green, faded signal red, cream shipping paper, dark teal water, salt-white glass, black cables, and low sodium dock light. The unofficial rule is that no section may restart merely because the audience expects a downbeat.

