# Fictional EP Tracklist

## 1. Seven Plucks Against Eight Lamps

**Story & Context:** The debut runs at 114 BPM. Mariam plays a seven-pulse simsimiyya figure built from an open fifth, two modal thirds, and a returning tonic. Jonas programs a restrained eight-beat dub-techno loop with one kick on each quarter note and a filtered minor-seventh chord every second bar. Because the cycles differ, the plucked phrase begins over a different electronic location each repetition.

Nadia sustains a prepared-harmonium fifth for eleven beats, creating a third recurrence length. Youssef contributes one three-stroke riqq figure every five bars. Lea enters only during the broadest gaps with low breath-led fifths.

At the midpoint, Jonas removes the kick for one full cycle but does not reset anything. When it returns, the acoustic and electronic layers are now perceived differently even though their actual phase relationship never changed. The track ends at a moment of near-alignment rather than waiting for every cycle to return simultaneously.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Night canal signal board with eight amber lamps above a paper strip showing seven repeated simsimiyya pluck marks, the two patterns visibly sliding against one another, oxidized brass and dark teal water beyond the window. No full lyrics; sparse spoken fragments limited to “seven against eight,” “do not restart,” “same speed,” and “meet later.”

## 2. Relay Room Echo Arrives One Step Early

**Story & Context:** At 118 BPM, Jonas sets two tape echoes to slightly different unsynced delay times. A short synth chord therefore produces returns that gradually shift relative to the four-on-the-floor kick. Mariam plays a nine-pulse simsimiyya cycle with muted ghost plucks between stronger notes.

Nadia uses two harmonium reeds tuned a close fifth apart and changes neither for the entire first half. Youssef inserts dry riqq rim taps only when the delay field becomes dense enough to obscure the original chord attack. Lea plays one sustained bass-flugelhorn note across three full simsimiyya cycles.

In the final section, Jonas reduces feedback until only the dry synth chord remains. The perceived complexity collapses, revealing that most of the motion had come from the two unsynchronized echo streams rather than additional notes.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Old relay cabinet with two tape-loop diagrams of slightly different lengths, one echo marker arriving just before an amber signal lamp, simsimiyya strings and riqq rim in shallow focus. Spoken fragments limited to “one step early,” “two delays,” “same chord moving,” and “dry signal remains.”

## 3. Canal Marker Seventeen Never Becomes Bar One

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 108 BPM. Mariam begins with an eleven-pulse simsimiyya figure while Jonas uses the simplest four-beat dub pulse on the EP. Nadia sustains a slow five-bar harmonium drone that changes only once. Youssef plays no continuous rhythm, only isolated muted riqq jingles separated by several bars.

Lea holds a low bass-flugelhorn pedal whenever the simsimiyya and synth chord happen to converge near the same tonal center. She does not count the meeting points in advance; she listens for them.

During the final three minutes, elements are removed one at a time without stopping the underlying cycles. First the hats disappear, then the synth chord, then the harmonium. The kick remains alone beneath Mariam’s eleven-pulse phrase. The track ends after canal marker seventeen is mentioned in a quiet spoken line, but no instrument returns to a designated “bar one.”

